==============================================================================================
WHAT THE 35 ARTICLES ARE, AND WHY NO SHARING PRACTICE CAN BE TESTED ON THEM
==============================================================================================
  35 of 35 matched into the coding data.
     data understandable        {'Yes': 35}
     analysis scripts shared    {'No': 34, 'Yes': 1}

  The 35 are CONSTANT on every sharing practice: all were judged to have all
  needed data and to be understandable -- that is the triage criterion -- and
  34 of 35 shared no analysis script. So this sample cannot test whether any
  sharing practice helps; the variable has no variance.
  What it CAN say is stronger and worse: this is the BEST-CASE stratum, hand
  triaged for 'a substantive finding based on a relatively straightforward
  analysis', and 13 of 35 (37%) could not be reproduced even with the
  original authors helping.

==============================================================================================
THE AVAILABILITY -> REUSABILITY FUNNEL, rebuilt from the raw coding data
==============================================================================================

  PRE-policy: 417 articles
    has a data-availability statement       104  (24.9% of articles)
    ...files actually downloaded and opened  103  (99.0% of statements)
    ...ALL needed data present                30  (28.8% of statements)   <- NOT PRINTED IN THE PAPER
    ...and understandable  = REUSABLE         23  (22.1% of statements, 5.5% of articles)
    of those that downloaded, also share an ANALYSIS SCRIPT: 9 (8.7%)

  POST-policy: 174 articles
    has a data-availability statement       136  (78.2% of articles)
    ...files actually downloaded and opened  133  (97.8% of statements)
    ...ALL needed data present               104  (76.5% of statements)   <- NOT PRINTED IN THE PAPER
    ...and understandable  = REUSABLE         85  (62.5% of statements, 48.9% of articles)
    of those that downloaded, also share an ANALYSIS SCRIPT: 8 (6.0%)

  Reproduces the paper exactly: pre 104/417 statements and 23/104 reusable;
  post 136/174 and 85/136. The two intermediate rungs are new: the policy's
  real effect is on COMPLETENESS, not on availability. Of articles whose data
  downloaded, the share with all needed data present goes 30/103 = 29%% to
  104/133 = 78%%. Availability was never the binding constraint.

==============================================================================================
THE END-TO-END NUMBER NOBODY STATES
==============================================================================================
  pre   P(reusable data) = 23/417 = 0.055   x  P(reproduces unaided | reusable) = 11/35 = 0.314   ->  1.7%
  post  P(reusable data) = 85/174 = 0.489   x  P(reproduces unaided | reusable) = 11/35 = 0.314   ->  15.4%

  A mandatory open-data policy moved the share of published articles whose
  reported numbers can be recovered from their own shared data, by a stranger,
  without contacting the author, from about 1.7% to about 15.4% -- a factor of
  8.9, and still about one article in 7.

  TWO ASSUMPTIONS, both mine and both load-bearing:
   1. The conditional 31.4%% is measured on 35 articles drawn from the
      REUSABLE pool and triaged further for 'a substantive finding based on a
      relatively straightforward analysis'. It is a BEST-CASE conditional.
   2. Applying it to the pre-policy era assumes the conditional did not change.
      If pre-policy reusable data was reusable for different reasons, it did.
  Both make the pre/post ratio a rough figure. The post-policy 15%% does not
  depend on assumption 2 and is the number I would defend.

